CHAPTER 3: SYSTEM DESIGN
3.1 Block Diagram
The system consists of the following main components:
HC-SR04 Ultrasonic Sensor → NodeMCU ESP8266 → Output Devices (LEDs and Buzzer).
The ultrasonic sensor measures the water level and sends the distance information to the NodeMCU. The microcontroller processes the information and activates the appropriate output devices.
3.2 Working Principle
The project works on the principle of ultrasonic distance measurement.
The HC-SR04 sensor sends ultrasonic waves towards the water surface. When these waves hit the water surface, they reflect back to the sensor.
The sensor calculates the distance using the time taken by the waves to return.
Distance Formula:
Distance = (Time × Speed of Sound) / 2
The distance is then used to calculate the water level percentage.
Water Level Percentage:
Water Level (%) = ((Tank Height − Measured Distance) / Tank Height) × 100
3.3 Circuit Connections
The connections between NodeMCU and other components are given below:
Component Pin	NodeMCU Pin
HC-SR04 VCC	VIN (5V supply)
HC-SR04 GND	GND
HC-SR04 TRIG	D1
HC-SR04 ECHO	D2 through voltage divider
Red LED	D5 through 220Ω resistor
Yellow LED	D6 through 220Ω resistor
Green LED	D7 through 220Ω resistor
Buzzer Positive	D0
Buzzer Negative	GND
Note: The HC-SR04 ECHO pin produces a 5V signal. A voltage divider using 1kΩ and 2kΩ resistors is used to reduce the voltage to approximately 3.3V before connecting it to the NodeMCU.
3.4 Water Level Indication
Water Level	LED Indication	Buzzer
0–29%	Red	OFF
30–69%	Yellow	OFF
70–89%	Green	OFF
90–100%	Green	ON

CHAPTER 4: IMPLEMENTATION
4.1 Implementation Steps
The project is implemented through the following steps:
Step 1: Component Collection
All the required electronic components, including NodeMCU, HC-SR04 sensor, LEDs, buzzer, resistors, and jumper wires, are collected.
Step 2: Hardware Connections
The ultrasonic sensor is connected to the NodeMCU. LEDs and buzzer are connected according to the circuit design.
Step 3: Software Installation
Arduino IDE is installed on the computer, and the ESP8266 board package is added.
Step 4: Programming
The program is written in Arduino C++ to read sensor data, calculate water-level percentage, and control the output devices.
Step 5: Code Uploading
The NodeMCU is connected to the computer using a USB cable, and the program is uploaded through Arduino IDE.
Step 6: Testing
The system is tested by gradually filling the water container and checking the LED indications and buzzer alert.
4.2 Algorithm
1.	Start the system.
2.	Initialize the ultrasonic sensor and output pins.
3.	Send an ultrasonic signal.
4.	Receive the reflected signal.
5.	Calculate the distance from the water surface.
6.	Calculate the water-level percentage.
7.	Check the water-level condition.
8.	Activate the corresponding LED.
9.	Activate the buzzer when the water level reaches 90%.
10.	Repeat the process continuously.
4.3 Software Implementation
Arduino IDE is used to write and upload the program to the NodeMCU ESP8266.
The program uses the following functions:
•	pinMode() – Defines input and output pins.
•	digitalWrite() – Controls LEDs and buzzer.
•	pulseIn() – Measures the duration of the ultrasonic echo.
•	delayMicroseconds() – Controls ultrasonic pulse timing.
•	Serial.print() – Displays sensor readings.
•	constrain() – Restricts the calculated percentage between 0 and 100.
